01:24:54.220 --> 01:24:56.800
could be through whatever means speaks to you

2091
01:24:56.800 --> 01:24:58.960
the loudest at this moment in time.

2092
01:24:58.960 --> 01:25:00.780
But I think that's what those kinds

2093
01:25:00.780 --> 01:25:03.440
of opportunities are for.

2094
01:25:03.440 --> 01:25:07.740
And it also exposes where we are living our lives

2095
01:25:07.740 --> 01:25:10.160
most unsustainably and feeling,

2096
01:25:10.160 --> 01:25:13.570
and perpetuating a sense of disconnection

2097
01:25:13.570 --> 01:25:15.050
and disunity with one another.

2098
01:25:15.050 --> 01:25:16.103
So, you know,

2099
01:25:16.103 --> 01:25:19.730
there's some opportunities within that as well as a society.

2100
01:25:19.730 --> 01:25:22.680
And I'm glad that that's become a part of the conversation.

2101
01:25:24.440 --> 01:25:27.770
What you said makes me feel hopeful.

2102
01:25:27.770 --> 01:25:29.620
But then I look at what's going on.

2103
01:25:29.620 --> 01:25:31.950
I see the divisiveness, I see the pain,

2104
01:25:31.950 --> 01:25:33.590
I see very real suffering.

2105
01:25:33.590 --> 01:25:36.610
A lot of people who are struggling,

2106
01:25:36.610 --> 01:25:38.930
and in meaningful ways with the challenges

2107
01:25:38.930 --> 01:25:42.150
that they've been presented with.

2108
01:25:42.150 --> 01:25:44.350
How do you grapple with, you know,

2109
01:25:44.350 --> 01:25:46.600
what is your perspective on

2110
01:25:46.600 --> 01:25:50.310
humanity's ability to emerge out of this situation?

2111
01:25:50.310 --> 01:25:54.480
Or just in general to thrive in a new and different way?

2112
01:25:54.480 --> 01:25:56.440
Yeah, we're in phase one, you know.

2113
01:25:56.440 --> 01:25:58.100
Like if I came in here for this interview

2114
01:25:58.100 --> 01:26:00.550
and I was like, rich, you know,

2115
01:26:00.550 --> 01:26:01.930
you did X, Y, and Z to me.

2116
01:26:01.930 --> 01:26:04.090
And you're like, what are you talking about?

2117
01:26:04.090 --> 01:26:05.780
I didn't do anything.

2118
01:26:05.780 --> 01:26:06.930
I don't know you like that.

2119
01:26:06.930 --> 01:26:08.450
And that's, those kinds of conversations

2120
01:26:08.450 --> 01:26:11.410
are happening with people where, you know,

2121
01:26:11.410 --> 01:26:14.260
you have one group of people who are feeling accused

2122
01:26:14.260 --> 01:26:16.070
of something that they don't feel

2123
01:26:16.070 --> 01:26:17.850
that they are directly responsible for.

2124
01:26:17.850 --> 01:26:20.070
And another group of people who feel victimized

2125
01:26:20.070 --> 01:26:22.430
or feel abused.

2126
01:26:22.430 --> 01:26:24.933
And so they're going through the stages of grief.

2127
01:26:25.940 --> 01:26:30.450
And so that's gonna create contention, you know,

2128
01:26:30.450 --> 01:26:32.640
in the middle, and you'd have to,

2129
01:26:32.640 --> 01:26:34.480
we have to move through that.

2130
01:26:34.480 --> 01:26:38.080
It happens with everything, it happened with me too, right.

2131
01:26:38.080 --> 01:26:40.720
It happens with all these different movements

2132
01:26:40.720 --> 01:26:45.720
where we kind of go in these extreme ways in the beginning,

2133
01:26:46.890 --> 01:26:49.270
but then eventually we kind of come back to the middle,

2134
01:26:49.270 --> 01:26:52.690
and we try to find unity in the middle.

2135
01:26:52.690 --> 01:26:57.380
And I think it's a good reminder that we're a lot more human

2136
01:26:57.380 --> 01:27:00.330
than we probably imagine ourselves to be.

2137
01:27:00.330 --> 01:27:03.930
All of us are, which is a good thing too,

2138
01:27:03.930 --> 01:27:06.750
'cause we're, I mean, we pulled the lens back further.

2139
01:27:06.750 --> 01:27:09.892
We're just animals, we're animals, who can think about

2140
01:27:09.892 --> 01:27:12.110
the future and the past more than say a monkey

2141
01:27:12.110 --> 01:27:14.140
or a dog or whatever can,

2142
01:27:14.140 --> 01:27:17.190
and we can organize a little bit better

2143
01:27:17.190 --> 01:27:18.690
than most other animals.

2144
01:27:18.690 --> 01:27:20.310
We're not, I mean, it's still

2145
01:27:21.350 --> 01:27:23.040
an argument of whether we're the most

2146
01:27:23.040 --> 01:27:25.290
intelligent creatures, right?

2147
01:27:25.290 --> 01:27:26.940
If you look at the way we treat each other,

2148
01:27:26.940 --> 01:27:29.510
that comes into serious question,

2149
01:27:29.510 --> 01:27:33.210
compared to whales and dolphins and you know,

2150
01:27:33.210 --> 01:27:34.043
things like that.

2151
01:27:34.043 --> 01:27:34.876
So,

2152
01:27:37.820 --> 01:27:39.290
I think this is a great first step.

2153
01:27:39.290 --> 01:27:40.300
At least we're talking about it,

2154
01:27:40.300 --> 01:27:42.690
because before this you had a whole segment

2155
01:27:42.690 --> 01:27:45.240
of the population who were feeling abused,

2156
01:27:45.240 --> 01:27:46.230
and there was no one

2157
01:27:46.230 --> 01:27:48.290
to talk to about it except for themselves.

2158
01:27:48.290 --> 01:27:49.790
So now we're talking about it,

2159
01:27:50.791 --> 01:27:53.570
and maybe by phase four or five,

2160
01:27:53.570 --> 01:27:56.210
you'll start to see a lot more unity

2161
01:27:56.210 --> 01:27:58.990
in these conversations instead of a lot of defensiveness.

2162
01:27:58.990 --> 01:28:01.540
Yeah, I mean, the manner in which change occurs,

2163
01:28:01.540 --> 01:28:04.210
transpires, is super interesting.

2164
01:28:04.210 --> 01:28:07.810
These changes always end up happening in the middle

2165
01:28:07.810 --> 01:28:10.070
through conversations of people

2166
01:28:10.070 --> 01:28:11.330
slowly coming together

2167
01:28:11.330 --> 01:28:13.810
and trying to identify what they actually agree on,

2168
01:28:13.810 --> 01:28:16.640
and concessions are made and all the like,

2169
01:28:16.640 --> 01:28:20.810
but the movements themselves are usually catalyzed

2170
01:28:20.810 --> 01:28:23.810
by people who don't want any,

2171
01:28:23.810 --> 01:28:25.080
have no interest in the middle.

2172
01:28:25.080 --> 01:28:28.500
Like they, it's through a very loud voice

2173
01:28:28.500 --> 01:28:31.210
that's taking a very intense stand

2174
01:28:31.210 --> 01:28:32.840
on a particular issue

2175
01:28:32.840 --> 01:28:34.870
that tends to garner people's attention,

2176
01:28:34.870 --> 01:28:37.150
and create the conversation in the first place.

2177
01:28:37.150 --> 01:28:40.550
So it's almost like you need those voices.

2178
01:28:40.550 --> 01:28:44.830
You need people who can do that, are willing to do that.

2179
01:28:44.830 --> 01:28:47.030
And then you need the negotiators,

2180
01:28:47.030 --> 01:28:49.770
and the the people in the middle as well.

2181
01:28:49.770 --> 01:28:52.710
I think it's like all voices are required.

2182
01:28:52.710 --> 01:28:55.340
Also if you look at Hollywood,

2183
01:28:55.340 --> 01:28:58.430
if you look at just media in general, right?

2184
01:28:58.430 --> 01:29:00.160
And I don't know this for fact,

2185
01:29:00.160 --> 01:29:01.250
but I'll just guesstimate

2186
01:29:01.250 --> 01:29:04.680
that maybe 90% of the stories that are told

2187
01:29:04.680 --> 01:29:06.650
are pretty much all about white people,

2188
01:29:06.650 --> 01:29:08.580
in every conceivable situation.

2189
01:29:08.580 --> 01:29:11.530
White people in space, white people in the future,

2190
01:29:11.530 --> 01:29:14.800
white people in the past, white people, you know,

2191
01:29:14.800 --> 01:29:15.983
doing all these things.

2192
01:29:17.279 --> 01:29:22.279
So naturally it breeds empathy for people,

2193
01:29:22.500 --> 01:29:24.190
white people in different situations,

2194
01:29:24.190 --> 01:29:27.520
but there are not that many stories about

2195
01:29:27.520 --> 01:29:30.810
the so-called other, about brown people in space.

2196
01:29:30.810 --> 01:29:34.140
No one's seen a movie about some black person on Neptune,

2197
01:29:34.140 --> 01:29:37.224
fighting with Matt Damon or something like that.

2198
01:29:37.224 --> 01:29:38.810
And so there's not,

2199
01:29:38.810 --> 01:29:42.700
it's harder to relate to these other people,

2200
01:29:42.700 --> 01:29:47.700
if your story is being told in every which way possible.

2201
01:29:48.830 --> 01:29:49.910
And then these other people are saying,

2202
01:29:49.910 --> 01:29:51.173
well, I'm just like you.

2203
01:29:51.173 --> 01:29:52.830
And it's like, well, you know, okay.

2204
01:29:52.830 --> 01:29:55.380
But I think what's happening now, which is great,

2205
01:29:55.380 --> 01:30:00.380
is that Hollywood is telling more stories of other people.

2206
01:30:00.480 --> 01:30:03.140
And the more we hear those songs,

2207
01:30:03.140 --> 01:30:04.390
the more we see those movies

2208
01:30:04.390 --> 01:30:07.340
and television shows and narratives,

2209
01:30:07.340 --> 01:30:09.980
the more people will start to relate

2210
01:30:09.980 --> 01:30:12.400
to these brown and black people

2211
01:30:12.400 --> 01:30:14.500
in all these different situations.

2212
01:30:14.500 --> 01:30:17.260
I think probably that is gonna bring us

2213
01:30:17.260 --> 01:30:19.540
closer together faster,

2214
01:30:19.540 --> 01:30:21.630
than just telling someone,

2215
01:30:21.630 --> 01:30:23.640
hey, I'm like you and I have these,

2216
01:30:23.640 --> 01:30:27.350
I'm human just like you, I have these dreams just like you.

2217
01:30:27.350 --> 01:30:30.962
And then having them kind of use their imagination.

2218
01:30:30.962 --> 01:30:33.670
We're a society, we have to actually see it.

2219
01:30:33.670 --> 01:30:36.773
We have to feel the empathy in that situation.

2220
01:30:39.313 --> 01:30:42.834
And then we kind of embody it in our life.

2221
01:30:42.834 --> 01:30:46.120
And I know you did a, you did a podcast with Ava DuVernay.

2222
01:30:46.120 --> 01:30:47.520
Is that something that you talked about with her?

2223
01:30:47.520 --> 01:30:49.170
I haven't listened to that yet.

2224
01:30:49.170 --> 01:30:52.610
Yeah, you know she talked about,

2225
01:30:52.610 --> 01:30:54.770
she's got this initiative called Hooray

2226
01:30:54.770 --> 01:30:59.770
where she is bringing more brown and black people

2227
01:31:00.040 --> 01:31:02.870
to the crews of these films.

2228
01:31:02.870 --> 01:31:05.100
So she's actually really passionate,

2229
01:31:05.100 --> 01:31:06.830
'cause she's obviously a director,

2230
01:31:06.830 --> 01:31:08.330
and she's done some wonderful work

2231
01:31:08.330 --> 01:31:09.993
in documentaries and film.

2232
01:31:11.420 --> 01:31:14.720
But in most of Hollywood productions,

2233
01:31:14.720 --> 01:31:16.210
even if it's a black movie,

2234
01:31:16.210 --> 01:31:20.040
the people behind the scenes are not mostly black,

2235
01:31:20.040 --> 01:31:21.640
and that can make a difference as well,

2236
01:31:21.640 --> 01:31:24.800
in the way that black people are filmed.

2237
01:31:24.800 --> 01:31:26.540
Like literally director of photography

2238
01:31:26.540 --> 01:31:29.630
may use different filters to bring out

2239
01:31:29.630 --> 01:31:33.760
the beauty and all of the nuances

2240
01:31:33.760 --> 01:31:35.520
of people with darker skin,

2241
01:31:35.520 --> 01:31:39.820
and those kinds of considerations aren't usually made in

2242
01:31:39.820 --> 01:31:42.140
in the executive suites of these studios.

2243
01:31:42.140 --> 01:31:44.110
So the more diversity,

2244
01:31:44.110 --> 01:31:46.180
and that's a big conversation now, as well,

2245
01:31:46.180 --> 01:31:48.240
is the more diversity you have in the boardroom

2246
01:31:48.240 --> 01:31:51.163
and the C suite, in the production,

2247
01:31:52.110 --> 01:31:55.480
the more valuable the product, the end result becomes,

2248
01:31:55.480 --> 01:31:57.310
because it's really speaking to more people.

2249
01:31:57.310 --> 01:31:59.760
So you're able to do more

2250
01:31:59.760 --> 01:32:00.890
of what you already want to do,

2251
01:32:00.890 --> 01:32:01.960
usually which is make money

2252
01:32:01.960 --> 01:32:03.840
and have influence and have impact.

2253
01:32:03.840 --> 01:32:05.050
You able to do more of that

2254
01:32:05.050 --> 01:32:06.580
by being more inclusive.

2255
01:32:06.580 --> 01:32:09.030
So that was kind her general message.

2256
01:32:09.030 --> 01:32:12.190
Yeah, and you're creating role models

2257
01:32:12.190 --> 01:32:14.280
within those communities

2258
01:32:14.280 --> 01:32:17.725
that lead to other-

2259
01:32:17.725 --> 01:32:21.225
(talking over each other)

2260
01:32:23.020 --> 01:32:24.370
And her story is awesome, man,

2261
01:32:24.370 --> 01:32:26.710
because she didn't start directly-

2262
01:32:26.710 --> 01:32:28.060
She was like a publicist, right?

2263
01:32:28.060 --> 01:32:29.040
She was a publicist.

2264
01:32:29.040 --> 01:32:33.590
She had a very successful publicist firm, a PR firm.

2265
01:32:33.590 --> 01:32:35.740
And then when she was 32,

2266
01:32:35.740 --> 01:32:37.600
she was on the set of "Collateral"

2267
01:32:37.600 --> 01:32:41.040
with Tom Cruise and Jamie Foxx.

2268
01:32:41.040 --> 01:32:42.400
And she said, you know, I could do this.

2269
01:32:42.400 --> 01:32:44.920
Michael Mann was directing, saying, I could do this.

2270
01:32:44.920 --> 01:32:48.110
And she started in her off time and weekends, holidays.

2271
01:32:48.110 --> 01:32:50.050
She started making these little short films,

2272
01:32:50.050 --> 01:32:53.900
and then that cascaded into her making a first feature.

2273
01:32:53.900 --> 01:32:57.036
Curiosity leading to tiny action.

2274
01:32:57.036 --> 01:32:58.170
She took the leap of faith, exactly.

2275
01:32:58.170 --> 01:33:00.690
But it started really small for her.

2276
01:33:00.690 --> 01:33:04.410
And she was like uniquely qualified to do the kind

2277
01:33:04.410 --> 01:33:06.040
of work that she was doing.

2278
01:33:06.040 --> 01:33:09.087
And that's just kind of going back to our original message

2279
01:33:09.087 --> 01:33:10.600
is that's what I'm telling people is that

2280
01:33:10.600 --> 01:33:12.820
you have some unique qualifications

2281
01:33:12.820 --> 01:33:16.040
that only you can do, and no one else in the world can do,

2282
01:33:16.040 --> 01:33:18.580
but you have to follow the inner blueprint

2283
01:33:18.580 --> 01:33:19.413
in order

2284
01:33:21.700 --> 01:33:23.443
to unveil them for the world.

2285
01:33:25.200 --> 01:33:26.870
It's like Ikea instructions,

2286
01:33:26.870 --> 01:33:29.230
like it looks confusing when you first look at it,

2287
01:33:29.230 --> 01:33:31.500
but if you just take it step by step by step,

2288
01:33:31.500 --> 01:33:34.620
and it all kinda comes together, and only you can do it.

2289
01:33:34.620 --> 01:33:36.730
But the difference would be

2290
01:33:36.730 --> 01:33:38.280
when you get the Ikea instructions,

2291
01:33:38.280 --> 01:33:40.030
all the instructions are there,

2292
01:33:40.030 --> 01:33:41.920
so you could read through them all the way to the end.

2293
01:33:41.920 --> 01:33:44.460
But in this case, you just get one instruction,

2294
01:33:44.460 --> 01:33:46.340
and trust that the next one,

2295
01:33:46.340 --> 01:33:48.310
when you complete it, there will be another one.

2296
01:33:48.310 --> 01:33:49.350
Right, and it may not,

2297
01:33:49.350 --> 01:33:50.830
you may get it underneath the toilet,

2298
01:33:50.830 --> 01:33:51.663
or you may get it

2299
01:33:51.663 --> 01:33:52.496
on the roof.
And it might be

2300
01:33:52.496 --> 01:33:53.329
in Swedish.

2301
01:33:53.329 --> 01:33:54.162
It might be in Swedish.

2302
01:33:54.162 --> 01:33:55.044
And you won't be able to read it.

2303
01:33:55.044 --> 01:33:55.877
Yeah, but you have to go to an interpreter,

2304
01:33:55.877 --> 01:33:57.790
and that interpreter ends up being your fiance,

2305
01:33:57.790 --> 01:34:00.240
'cause you know, you have a connection and it's like

2306
01:34:00.240 --> 01:34:02.000
everything is so beautiful.

2307
01:34:02.000 --> 01:34:03.376
It's almost like a beautiful-

2308
01:34:03.376 --> 01:34:06.990
Yeah, it's like a beautiful little game

2309
01:34:06.990 --> 01:34:09.123
that all kind of comes together, a puzzle.

2310
01:34:10.090 --> 01:34:11.890
But when you look back at the end of life,

2311
01:34:11.890 --> 01:34:13.550
and that's what I love about what Steve Jobs said,

2312
01:34:13.550 --> 01:34:15.190
you can't connect the dots, looking forward.

2313
01:34:15.190 --> 01:34:16.390
You can only connect them looking back.

2314
01:34:16.390 --> 01:34:17.640
When you look back at the end of life,

2315
01:34:17.640 --> 01:34:19.210
everything kind of-

2316
01:34:19.210 --> 01:34:21.430
It's crazy how that works and the older I get.

2317
01:34:21.430 --> 01:34:22.810
Everything connects.

2318
01:34:22.810 --> 01:34:25.900
And I reflect back and I'm like, it all makes sense.

2319
01:34:25.900 --> 01:34:29.850
And when you're in it, it's utterly confusing and baffling.

2320
01:34:29.850 --> 01:34:30.683
And it makes no sense.

2321
01:34:30.683 --> 01:34:32.240
And you think you're the only one who's confused,

2322
01:34:32.240 --> 01:34:36.350
but when you get older, you realize everybody's confused.

2323
01:34:36.350 --> 01:34:38.130
Nobody knows what they're doing.

2324
01:34:38.130 --> 01:34:38.963
Nobody.

2325
01:34:38.963 --> 01:34:40.700
And that's another great thing about the pandemic

2326
01:34:40.700 --> 01:34:43.180
os it exposed the fact that even at

2327
01:34:43.180 --> 01:34:45.280
the highest levels of government,

2328
01:34:45.280 --> 01:34:47.050
nobody knew what they were doing.

2329
01:34:47.050 --> 01:34:48.625
You know, we're all just-

2330
01:34:48.625 --> 01:34:49.710
(talking over each other)

2331
01:34:49.710 --> 01:34:51.070
You just think like the adults,

2332
01:34:51.070 --> 01:34:52.520
they know what they're doing, they're in control.

2333
01:34:52.520 --> 01:34:53.980
They got it covered.

2334
01:34:53.980 --> 01:34:55.407
I was talking to my daughter the other day,

2335
01:34:55.407 --> 01:34:58.280
and I was like, 'cause she's she's 17 now.

2336
01:34:58.280 --> 01:34:59.720
And I was like,

2337
01:34:59.720 --> 01:35:02.270
it must be so disappointing to come in,

2338
01:35:02.270 --> 01:35:05.033
when you first realized like adults don't know shit.

2339
01:35:06.400 --> 01:35:08.340
She was laughing, you know?

2340
01:35:08.340 --> 01:35:10.580
'Cause when you're 17, you think you know it,

2341
01:35:10.580 --> 01:35:11.940
you think you know it all

2342
01:35:11.940 --> 01:35:13.888
like I didn't hit on that part of it.

2343
01:35:13.888 --> 01:35:17.010
But you are developing an awareness that all these people

2344
01:35:17.010 --> 01:35:20.186
that you thought had things managed,

2345
01:35:20.186 --> 01:35:22.760
there's a lot of fuck-ups out there, too.

2346
01:35:22.760 --> 01:35:27.100
And that I think fuels that adolescent or teenage

2347
01:35:27.100 --> 01:35:29.020
